<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Klokotnitsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1230">1230</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Klokotnitsa was fought on 9 March 1230 near the village of Klokotnitsa in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> routed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, who had broken a treaty and invaded Bulgaria, and took him prisoner. The victory ended Epirote power and brought much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> under Bulgarian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa.jpg" alt="Battle of Klokotnitsa" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kandi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Klokotnitsa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Around 1221 to 1222 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. Secure on that side, Theodore took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> and lands in Macedonia including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, and established the Empire of Thessalonica. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor Robert of Courtenay died in 1228, Ivan Asen II was thought the likeliest regent for the young Baldwin II. Theodore saw Bulgaria as the last obstacle on his road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and invaded it early in March 1230 without a declaration of war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Theodore gathered a large army that included western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and was so sure of victory that he brought his whole court, his wife and children among them. His army moved slowly, plundering villages on its way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> raised a small force of a few thousand men, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> among them, and marched south so fast that in four days he covered three times the distance Theodore had travelled in a week. It is said that he carried the broken treaty fixed to his spear as a banner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> surrounded the surprised enemy, and the fighting went on until sunset. Only a small force under Theodore's brother Manuel escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> released the captured soldiers without conditions and took the nobles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>. His name as a merciful ruler went before him, and the lands Theodore had recently won in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> without resistance, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> became a Bulgarian vassal under Manuel. In the Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs at Tarnovo the tsar had an inscription carved on a marble column recording that he had defeated the Greek army and captured their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>, Kyr Teodore Komnenos, together with all his boyars, and conquered his land from Adrianople to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>. Bulgaria emerged once again as the strongest state of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Klokotnitsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Epirote threat ended with Epirus's defeat by the Bulgarians at the <b>Battle of Klokotnitsa</b>, but Nicaea took its place.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His half-brother Theodore expanded the state, taking Thessalonica in 1224 and creating the Empire of Thessalonica, until his defeat by the Bulgarians at <b>Klokotnitsa</b> in 1230.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bulgarian tsar Ivan Asen II absorbed his lands around 1230, after defeating Epirus at <b>Klokotnitsa</b>, into the Second Bulgarian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-melnik">Despotate of Melnik</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1230 he attacked Bulgaria but was defeated and captured by Ivan Asen II at <b>Klokotnitsa</b> and later blinded.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a></li></ul></section>
